L000293	\section{Symmetries of the Lindblad Master Equation}
L000294	
L000295	We briefly discuss the symmetries associated with the Lindblad master equation and the jump operators defined in the main text. In addition to a standard weak $U(1)$ symmetry,
L000296	We briefly discuss the symmetries associated with the Lindblad master equation and the jump operators defined in the main text. In addition to a standard weak $U(1)$ symmetry, where the fermionic operators can be rotated by an arbitrary global phase $c_j\rightarrow e^{i\alpha}c_j$ without changing the master equation, the system also features a local gauge symmetry associated with the phases $\phi,\theta$. In particular under PBC and local transformation
L000297	\begin{equation}
L000298	c_j\rightarrow e^{i r_j\theta }c_{j}, \quad c^{\dagger}_j\rightarrow e^{-i r_j\theta }c^{\dagger}_{j}
L000299	\end{equation}
L000300	where $r_j$ is the position of the site $j$, one can eliminate the phase $\theta$ from the gain term, whose jump operators transform as
L000301	\begin{align}
L000302	L_{j,g}\rightarrow \sqrt{\Gamma}e^{-i r_j\theta}\left(c^{\dagger}_j+c^{\dagger}_{j+1}\right),
L000303	\end{align}
L000304	giving rise to a gain-dissipator which is independent of $\theta$. At the same time, the loss term acquires a $\theta$ dependence,
L000305	\begin{align}
L000306	L_{j,\ell}\rightarrow \sqrt{\kappa}e^{ir_j\theta}\left(c_j+e^{i(\phi+\theta)}c_{j+1}\right),
L000307	\end{align}
L000308	and similarly, the hopping term acquires a Peierls-like phase
L000309	\begin{equation}
L000310	-\dfrac{J}{2}\left( c^{\dagger}_jc_{j+1}+\text{h.c.}\right)\rightarrow
L000311	-\dfrac{J}{2}\left( e^{i\theta}c^{\dagger}_jc_{j+1}+\text{h.c.}\right).
L000312	\end{equation}
L000313	In other words, our model with non-reciprocal gain and losses is equivalent to a model with momentum-dependent (but reciprocal) gain, non-reciprocal losses with a phase $\theta+\phi$ and non-reciprocal hopping due to a Peierls phase $e^{i\theta}$. However, we note that, while the phase $\theta$ can be eliminated from the Lindbladian, it still affects physical observables which need to be transformed as well.
L000314	Finally, we stress that our model in the absence of interaction $\Delta=0$ has particle-hole symmetry for $\Gamma=\kappa$ and $\phi=\theta$ since in this case the master equation is invariant under $c_j\leftrightarrow c^{\dagger}_j$. A finite $\Delta$ gives rise to a shift in the Hamiltonian $\delta H=-2\Delta \sum_i n_i$.
L000315	
L000316	
L000317	\section{Non-Interacting Case: Equations of Motion and Continuity Equation}
L000318	
L000319	In this section, we discuss in more detail the non-interacting limit ($\Delta=0$). We provide additional details that support the results discussed in the main text for both open-boundary and periodic boundary conditions. First, we write the equation of motion for the point correlation function,
L000320	\begin{equation}
L000321	\begin{aligned}i\partial_{t}\left\langle c_{n}^{\dagger}c_{m}\right\rangle  & =-\dfrac{1}{2}\left[J+i\kappa e^{i\phi}+i\Gamma e^{-i\theta}\right]\left\langle c_{n}^{\dagger}c_{m+1}\right\rangle -\dfrac{1}{2}\left[J+i\kappa e^{-i\phi}+i\Gamma e^{i\theta}\right]\left\langle c_{n}^{\dagger}c_{m-1}\right\rangle +\\
L000322	 & +\dfrac{1}{2}\left[J-i\kappa e^{-i\phi}-i\Gamma e^{i\theta}\right]\left\langle c_{n+1}^{\dagger}c_{m}\right\rangle +\dfrac{1}{2}\left[J-i\kappa e^{i\phi}-i\Gamma e^{-i\theta}\right]\left\langle c_{n-1}^{\dagger}c_{m}\right\rangle+\\
L000323	 & -2i\kappa\left\langle c_{n}^{\dagger}c_{m}\right\rangle +2i\Gamma\left(\delta_{n,m}-\left\langle c_{n}^{\dagger}c_{m}\right\rangle \right)+\\
L000324	 & +i\dfrac{\Gamma}{2} \left(e^{-i\theta}\left[\delta_{n,m+1}+\delta_{n-1,m}\right]+e^{i\theta}\left[\delta_{n,m-1}+\delta_{n+1,m}\right] \right).
L000325	\end{aligned}
L000326	\label{eq:equation_of_motion_no_interacting_case}
L000327	\end{equation}
L000328	As already discussed, both gain and loss dissipation couple to the hopping elements of the unitary evolution, leading to the appearance of two distinct effective hopping rates: $J_\pm=J + i\kappa e^{\pm i \phi} + i\Gamma e^{\mp i\theta}$.
L000329	
L000330	It is interesting to see how these effective couplings affect the dynamics of the particle density at a site $n$,
L000331	\begin{equation}
L000332	\partial_{t}\left\langle c_{n}^{\dagger}c_{n}\right\rangle + v_+ \langle \mathcal{I}_{n}\rangle - v_-\langle\mathcal{I}_{n-1} \rangle+ \dfrac{\langle h_{n}\rangle + \langle h_{n-1} \rangle}{2J}\left[\kappa\cos\left(\phi\right)+\Gamma\cos\left(\theta\right)\right]=\langle \mathcal{T}_{n} \rangle,
L000333	\label{eq:continuity_equation}
L000334	\end{equation}
L000335	where $ v_\pm = 1\pm\left[\Gamma/J\sin\left(\theta\right)-\kappa/J\sin\left(\phi\right)\right]$, $\langle \mathcal{T}_n \rangle =2\Gamma-2\left(\Gamma+\kappa \right)\langle c^\dagger_n c_n \rangle$ which is the global rate of particle injection/losses from the bath, $\langle \mathcal{I}_n\rangle$ the expectation value of the local particle current operator at a site $n$ and $\langle h_n\rangle$ the expectation value of the local kinetic energy density at a site $n$, namely,
L000336	\begin{equation}
L000337	\langle \mathcal{I}_{n} \rangle=\dfrac{iJ}{2}\left(\left\langle c_{n+1}^{\dagger}c_{n}\right\rangle -\left\langle c_{n}^{\dagger}c_{n+1}\right\rangle \right), \quad\langle  h_{n} \rangle  = \langle c_{n+1}^{\dagger}c_{n}\rangle  +  \langle c_{n}^{\dagger}c_{n+1}\rangle.
L000338	\end{equation}
L000339	Eq.~\eqref{eq:continuity_equation} would describe a local continuity equation associated with global conservation of the total particle number if the system were governed solely by Hamiltonian dynamics. However, the right-hand side of the equation reveals that particles can be injected into or removed from the system due to coupling with the bath. In particular, the imaginary components of the effective hoppings $J_\pm$, renormalize and induce direction-dependent effective velocities. This is similar to what was observed in certain regimes for the no-click dynamics of the Hatano-Nelson model~\cite{Soares2024Faber}. In addition, their real part introduces a non-trivial contribution that depends on the local kinetic energy.
L000340	
L000341	As we show next, an imbalance in $v_\pm$ serves as a reliable indicator of directionality in the propagation of the local particle density. This is further evident from the local particle current in the PBC case, where, despite the absence of directional propagation - since the system ultimately reaches homogeneity - directionality can still be inferred from the presence of a net particle current.
L000342	
L000343	\subsection{Results for Periodic Boundary Conditions}
L000344	\begin{figure}[t]
L000345	    \centering
L000346	    \includegraphics{Figs/s1.pdf}
L000347	    \caption{Upper row - Steady-state distribution for different values of the angle $\theta$ with $\phi$ fixed with $\Gamma=\kappa$. Lower row - Steady-state particle current as a function of the ratio $\Gamma/\kappa$ for different values of the angle $\theta$ with $\phi$ fixed. The $\Gamma/\kappa$ axis is in log-scale.}
L000348	    \label{fig:steady_state_nk_current_pbc_vs_ratio}
L000349	\end{figure}
L000350	Here we provide further analytical results for the PBC case in the absence of interactions. In this case, the equation of motion for the correlation matrix Eq.~\eqref{eq:equation_of_motion_no_interacting_case}, when written in momentum space $C_{kq}=\left\langle c_{k}^{\dagger}c_{q}\right\rangle$, simplify to
L000351	\begin{equation}
L000352	\partial_{t}C_{kq} =-\left[i\left(\varepsilon_k -\varepsilon_q \right)+\dfrac{\lambda_k+\lambda_q}{2} \right]C_{kq} +\delta_{k,q}\: \Gamma_k,
L000353	\end{equation}
L000354	with $\lambda_k=\Gamma_k + \kappa_k$ as defined in the main text, and $\varepsilon_k=-J\cos\left(k \right)$. The solution corresponds, for the off-diagonal matrix elements, to an exponential decay in time,
L000355	\begin{equation}
L000356	C_{kq}(t)=C_{kq}(0)e^{-i\left(\varepsilon_k -\varepsilon_q \right)t}e^{-(\lambda_k+\lambda_q)t/2},
L000357	\end{equation}
L000358	while, for the diagonal elements of the correlation matrix (that is the distribution function $n_k(t)=C_{kk}(t)$), we obtain Eq.~(6) of the main text.
L000359	First, we discuss the steady-state solution of these equations of motion. In the steady-state, the correlation matrix is diagonal in momentum; as such, the steady-state particle density and current are spatially uniform and given by
L000360	\begin{equation}
L000361	\left\langle n_j\right\rangle_{\text{ss}} =\int_{-\pi}^{+\pi}\dfrac{dk}{2\pi} \langle n_{k} \rangle_{\text{ss}},\qquad
L000362	\left\langle \mathcal{I}_{j}\right\rangle_{\text{ss}}
L000363	=-J\int_{-\pi}^{+\pi}\dfrac{dk}{2\pi}\sin\left(k\right) \langle n_{k} \rangle_{\text{ss}},
L000364	\end{equation}
L000365	
L000366	where the steady-state distribution is given in Eq.~(8)
L000367	of the main text, that we rewrite here as a function of the angles $\theta,\phi$ and the ratio between the gain and loss rate $\kappa/\Gamma$
L000368	\begin{equation}\label{eqn:distribution}
L000369	\langle n_{k} \rangle_{\text{ss}} =\dfrac{\left(1+\cos\left(k-\theta\right)\right)}{\left(1+\cos\left(k-\theta\right)\right)+\dfrac{\kappa}{\Gamma}\left(1+\cos\left(\phi+k\right)\right)}.
L000370	\end{equation}
L000371	
L000372	
L000373	In Fig.~\ref{fig:steady_state_nk_current_pbc_vs_ratio}, we plot the distribution function $\langle n_{k}\rangle_{\text{ss}}$ for different values of the angles (top row) and the corresponding particle current as a function of $\Gamma/\kappa$ and the two angles (bottom row). As mentioned in the main text, the steady state does not support a particle current when the momentum distribution $\langle n_k \rangle_{\rm ss}$ is flat in $k$,
L000374	which occurs along the line $\phi=-\theta$. In this case, as seen from Eq.~\eqref{eqn:distribution}, the two dissipative processes interfere destructively and cancel out, leaving a distribution function $\langle n_{k}\rangle_{\text{ss}}=\Gamma/(\Gamma+\kappa)$, which is identical to the case of local onsite gain ($L_{j,g}=\sqrt{\Gamma}c^\dagger_{j}$) and losses ($L_{j,\ell}=\sqrt{\kappa}c_{j}$). In contrast, the particle current is maximized when $\langle n_{k}\rangle_{\text{ss}} = (1 + \sin(k))/2$, which occurs for $\theta = \phi = \pm\pi/2$ and $\Gamma = \kappa$, as clearly seen in the lower row of Fig.~\ref{fig:steady_state_nk_current_pbc_vs_ratio} and in Fig.~(2).(b)
L000375	of the main text. In this case, the modes with the highest group velocity are the most populated, resulting in a maximal current. As the angles are tuned away from this line, the distribution function loses the (anti)symmetry with respect to inversion, thus reducing the current. Moreover, as shown in the figure, the particle current vanishes in both limits $\Gamma/\kappa \to 0$ and $\Gamma/\kappa \to \infty$, corresponding to a fully empty and fully filled chain, respectively, and reaches its maximum at $\Gamma = \kappa$. The sign of the current is controlled by both angles but also by the gain/loss ratio. This is seen in Fig.~\ref{fig:steady_state_nk_current_pbc_vs_ratio}.(e), where for some angles $\theta$ and a fixed $\phi=\pi/2$, the current can be reversed by increasing the gain/loss ratio. When $\theta=\phi$, we can derive an analytically expression for the particle current, also assuming that $\Gamma=\kappa$,
L000376	\begin{equation}
L000377	\langle\mathcal{I}_{j}\rangle_{\text{ss}} = J\dfrac{\sin(\theta) }{2\cos^2(\theta)}\left(|\sin(\theta)|-1 \right),
L000378	\end{equation}
L000379	in this case, the direction of the particle current is given by the sign of the $\sin\left(\theta\right)$.
L000380	
L000381	
L000382	
L000383	\begin{figure}[t]
L000384	    \centering
L000385	    \includegraphics{Figs/s2.pdf}
L000386	    \caption{a) - Absolute value of the two-point correlation function as a function of distance $r$ for $\phi=\pi/4$. The correlation length is minimal and equal to zero for $\theta=-\phi$. b) - Inverse correlation length as a function as function of $\theta$ for two distinct values of $\phi$. Other parameters: $\Gamma=\kappa=0.1J$}
L000387	    \label{fig:steady_state_correlation_length}
L000388	\end{figure}
L000389	
L000390	It is also interesting to explore other correlation functions. We note that the steady state is never critical: the steady-state two-point correlation function always decays exponentially, $\langle c^\dagger_{j} c_{j+r} \rangle \propto e^{-r/\xi}$, with a correlation length $\xi$ that remains finite throughout the phase diagram for all system parameters. This correlation length depends sensitively on the angles and exhibits a non-monotonic dependence as one angle is varied relative to the other, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:steady_state_correlation_length}.(a). This can be analytically determined through the poles of the distribution $\langle n_k \rangle_{\text{ss}}$, which indicate that
L000391	\begin{equation}
L000392	    \xi^{-1}= \dfrac{1}{2}\log\left(\dfrac{\Gamma+\kappa+2\sqrt{\Gamma\kappa}\left|\sin\left(\dfrac{\theta+\phi}{2}\right)\right|}{\Gamma+\kappa-2\sqrt{\Gamma\kappa}\left|\sin\left(\dfrac{\theta+\phi}{2}\right)\right|} \right).
L000393	\end{equation}
L000394	We note that this expression is not valid for $\theta=-\phi$. As one approaches the line $\theta=-\phi$ the dissipative gap $\delta$ decreases toward zero, leading to an increasing correlation length. However, right at the critical line, $\delta=0$, also the gain vanishes in the same way, giving rise to a completely flat momentum distribution in $k$ space, i.e. $\langle c^\dagger_{j} c_{j+r} \rangle=\delta_{r,0}$.
L000395	
L000396	
L000397	
L000398	
L000399	
L000400	For what concerns the transient dynamics, we first consider an initially empty lattice such that the dynamics of particle density and current does not depend on initial off diagonal correlations in momentum space. As such, the particle density and coherent current at a site $j$ can be formally obtained by using,
L000401	\begin{align}
L000402	\left\langle n_j(t)\right\rangle  =\int_{-\pi}^{+\pi}\dfrac{dk}{2\pi} \langle n_{k}(t) \rangle,\,\qquad
L000403	\left\langle \mathcal{I}_{j}(t)\right\rangle
L000404	=-J\int_{-\pi}^{+\pi}\dfrac{dk}{2\pi}\sin\left(k\right) \langle n_{k}(t) \rangle,
L000405	\end{align}
L000406	where the time dependent distribution function is given in Eq.~(6) of the main text.
L000407	We start by considering the line $\phi=-\theta$, for which we have $\lambda_k = 2\left(\Gamma+\kappa\right)\left[1+\cos\left(k+\phi\right)\right]$. In this case we can rewrite the particle density as
L000408	\begin{equation}
L000409	\begin{aligned}
L000410	\left\langle n_j(t)\right\rangle
L000411	 & =\dfrac{\Gamma}{\Gamma+\kappa}\left(1-e^{-2\left(\Gamma+\kappa\right)t}\int_{-\pi}^{+\pi}\dfrac{dk}{2\pi}e^{-2\left(\Gamma+\kappa\right)\cos\left(k+\phi\right)t}\right)\\
L000412	 & =\dfrac{\Gamma}{\Gamma+\kappa}\left(1-e^{-2\left(\Gamma+\kappa\right)t}\sum_{n=-\infty}^{+\infty}\int_{-\pi}^{+\pi}\dfrac{dk}{2\pi}\left(-ie^{i(k+\phi)}\right)^{n}J_{n}\left(2i\left(\Gamma+\kappa\right)t\right)\right)\\
L000413	 & =\dfrac{\Gamma}{\Gamma+\kappa}\left(1-e^{-2\left(\Gamma+\kappa\right)t}I_{0}\left(2\left(\Gamma+\kappa\right)t\right)\right),
L000414	 \end{aligned}
L000415	 \label{eq:density_pbc_vaccum}
L000416	\end{equation}
L000417	where $I_{0}(\cdot)$ is the zero order Bessel Function of the second kind. Similarly, for the current, we get
L000418	\begin{equation}
L000419	\langle\mathcal{I}_{j}\left(t\right)\rangle  =-\dfrac{\Gamma}{\Gamma+\kappa} I_{1}\left(2(\Gamma + \kappa) t\right)e^{-2(\Gamma + \kappa) t}\sin\left(\phi\right),
L000420	\end{equation}
L000421	with $I_{1}(\cdot)$ the first order Bessel Function of the second kind.
L000422	As mentioned in the main text, we see that there is a transient current as long as $\phi\neq n\pi$, even though in the steady-state the current vanishes. We also see that the direction is controlled by the angle $\phi$.
L000423	
L000424	
L000425	In the limit $t\rightarrow + \infty$, we can expand the Bessel functions and obtain the asymptotics of particle density and current
L000426	\begin{equation}
L000427	\begin{aligned}
L000428	\left\langle n_{j} (t\rightarrow\infty)\right\rangle &= \frac{\Gamma}{\Gamma+\kappa} \left(1-\frac{1}{2\sqrt{\pi}\sqrt{\Gamma+\kappa}}\sqrt{\frac{1}{t}}\right) + O\left(t^{-3/2}\right),\\
L000429	\langle\mathcal{I}_{j} (t\rightarrow\infty)\rangle &= \dfrac{\Gamma}{2\sqrt{\pi}(\Gamma+\kappa)^{3/2}} \sin\left(\phi\right) \sqrt{\frac{1}{t}}+O\left(t^{-3/2}\right).
L000430	\end{aligned}
L000431	\end{equation}
L000432	In both cases, we obtain a power-law decay towards the steady-state value (which is zero for the current) of the form $1/t^{\alpha}$ with an exponent $\alpha=1/2$. This decay is ultimately due to the fact that the spectrum of decay rates $\lambda_k$ vanishes quadratically around $k_*$. On the other hand, away from the line $\theta=-\phi$, the spectrum $\lambda_k$ is gapped, $\lambda_k=\delta+\alpha(k-k_*)^2$ and the dynamics of both particle density and current acquire an additional exponential decay with a scale controlling the distance from the critical line $\phi=-\theta$.
